(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-acid-base-part-1-quiz"]={"title":"Acid-Base Part I Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which saline-unresponsive cause of metabolic alkalosis comes with hypertension?","choices":["Hypoparathyroidism with hypercalcemia","Diuretic therapy","Nasogastric suction","Primary aldosteronism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypertensive saline-unresponsive alkalosis comes from primary aldosteronism, mineralocorticoids and licorice. The normotensive causes are severe potassium depletion and hypoparathyroidism with hypercalcemia. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What can follow rapid correction of chronic respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Post-hypocapnia metabolic acidosis","Posthypercapnic metabolic alkalosis","Hyperkalemia from potassium release","Respiratory acidosis from hypoventilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid correction of chronic respiratory alkalosis may result in a post-hypocapnia metabolic acidosis. The kidneys had lowered the bicarbonate during compensation, so a fast fix leaves it low. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"By the Winter formula, what is the expected carbon dioxide when bicarbonate is 12 mEq/L?","choices":["16 to 20 mm Hg","24 to 28 mm Hg","30 to 34 mm Hg","36 to 44 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x bicarbonate + 8, plus or minus 2. With bicarbonate 12: 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 18 + 8 = 26, so the expected range is 24 to 28 mm Hg. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which is a listed cause of error in interpreting the anion gap?","choices":["Hypoalbuminemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperglycemia","Anemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Causes for error in anion gap interpretation are hypoalbuminemia, hypernatremia or hyponatremia, and antibiotics. A normal gap does not exclude acidosis and should not override clinical concern. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"In acidosis, which shift explains hyperkalemia?","choices":["Bicarbonate carries potassium into blood","Hydrogen moves into cells and potassium moves out","Potassium moves into cells and hydrogen moves out","The kidney retains extra potassium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In acidosis, hydrogen ions shift from the extracellular to the intracellular fluid, and potassium shifts from intracellular to extracellular fluid, which produces hyperkalemia. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which term describes an arterial pH below 7.35?","choices":["Alkalemia","Hypercapnia","Acidemia","Acidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Normal arterial pH is 7.35 to 7.45, so a value below 7.35 is acidemia, the blood finding. Acidosis is the pathologic condition of excessively acidic body fluids or tissues that produces it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which oxygen strategy is used in chronic respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Careful oxygen titration","High-flow oxygen to 100%","Withholding oxygen entirely","Oxygen during sleep alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic respiratory acidosis is managed by treating the cause, using careful oxygen titration and avoiding rapid or aggressive carbon dioxide reduction (which risks posthypercapnic metabolic alkalosis). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which neurologic finding is listed for respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Asterixis","Perioral numbness","Tetany","Hyperreflexia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory acidosis causes somnolence, confusion, altered mental status, asterixis and myoclonus. Severe hypercapnia can also raise cerebral blood flow and intracranial pressure, with papilledema. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"In metabolic alkalosis, a carbon dioxide above what level suggests a concurrent primary respiratory acidosis?","choices":["40 mm Hg","45 mm Hg","55 mm Hg","80 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory compensation for metabolic alkalosis rarely raises the carbon dioxide above 55 mm Hg. A higher value suggests a concurrent primary respiratory acidosis rather than compensation alone. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is metabolic alkalosis compensated?","choices":["Increased ventilation to blow off carbon dioxide","Renal retention of bicarbonate","Renal excretion of ammonium chloride","Decreased ventilation to retain carbon dioxide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic alkalosis is compensated by decreased ventilation, which retains carbon dioxide. The carbon dioxide rarely rises above 55 mm Hg from compensation alone, so higher suggests a second disorder. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 22, 38, 59"},{"q":"Which symptom is typical of respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Perioral numbness","Somnolence","Asterixis","Papilledema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory alkalosis causes light-headedness, anxiety, perioral numbness and paresthesias. In severe cases tetany from low calcium occurs, because a high pH increases calcium binding with albumin. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What happens to carbon dioxide when ventilation increases?","choices":["It increases","It stays the same","It turns into bicarbonate","It decreases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Increased ventilation decreases carbon dioxide, and decreased ventilation increases it. In the steady state, carbon dioxide production matches excretion, so changing ventilation shifts the balance. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 12, 22, 30"},{"q":"Carbonic acid dissociates into which two ions?","choices":["Sodium and chloride","Potassium and hydrogen","Ammonium and chloride","Hydrogen and bicarbonate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic acid dissociates into hydrogen ions and bicarbonate ions, and the reaction can run in reverse. That is why the buffer can be shifted from the respiratory side (carbon dioxide) or the renal side (bicarbonate). — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which triad defines obesity hypoventilation (Pickwickian) syndrome?","choices":["Obesity, hyperventilation, hypocapnia","Daytime sleepiness, snoring, normal carbon dioxide","Obesity, sleep disordered breathing, chronic hypercapnia","Obesity, hypertension, hyperglycemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome, also called Pickwickian syndrome, is the triad of obesity, sleep disordered breathing and chronic hypercapnia. It is a cause of chronic respiratory acidosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the treatment of metabolic acidosis, whether the anion gap is normal or elevated?","choices":["Give isotonic saline for every case","Stop the diuretics","Treat the underlying disorder","Breathe into a paper bag"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For both increased and normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, the treatment is to treat the underlying disorder. The compensatory response is also assessed, using the Winter formula. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 35, 44, 56, 57"},{"q":"To check compensation in a primary respiratory disorder, which expected value is calculated?","choices":["The expected urine chloride","The expected bicarbonate","The expected carbon dioxide","The expected anion gap"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For a respiratory disorder, compensation is renal, so the expected bicarbonate is checked. For a metabolic disorder, compensation is respiratory, so the expected carbon dioxide is checked. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How does alkalosis change hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen?","choices":["Increases it, so tissues get less oxygen","Decreases it, so tissues get more oxygen","Has no effect on affinity","Increases it, so tissues get more oxygen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alkalosis increases hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen, so less oxygen is released and delivery to the tissues decreases. This is the leftward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which toxic or pharmacologic agent causes respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Licorice","Salicylates","Intravenous sedation","Recreational opioids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The toxic and pharmacologic causes of respiratory alkalosis are salicylates, caffeine, nicotine, catecholamines and thyroxine. Each drives hyperventilation, which lowers carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which sequence produces respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Bicarbonate loss, low bicarbonate, acidemia","Vomiting, raised bicarbonate, alkalemia","Hypoventilation, raised carbon dioxide, acidemia","Hyperventilation, low carbon dioxide, alkalemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory acidosis is hypoventilation leading to an elevated arterial carbon dioxide (above 40 mm Hg) and acidemia. It may be accompanied by hypoxemia and may be acute or chronic. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which type of metabolic alkalosis is more common?","choices":["Saline unresponsive","Both are equally common","Neither; most are respiratory","Saline responsive"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic alkalosis is saline responsive or saline unresponsive, and the saline-responsive type is the more common. It signals extracellular volume contraction and improves with isotonic saline. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which eye finding may appear with severe hypercapnia?","choices":["Cotton-wool spots","Conjunctival pallor","A fixed dilated pupil","Papilledema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe hypercapnia increases cerebral blood flow, intracranial pressure and cerebrospinal fluid pressure, and papilledema may be seen. The eye finding reflects the raised pressure. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the anion gap formula?","choices":["1.5 x bicarbonate + 8","Sodium - (chloride + bicarbonate)","(Sodium + potassium) - chloride","Chloride - (sodium + bicarbonate)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The anion gap equals sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate, using serum electrolyte values. The normal anion gap is 9 to 15, and serum albumin accounts for most of it. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which pulmonary cause is listed for respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Airway obstruction","Aspiration pneumonitis","Pulmonary embolism","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary causes of respiratory alkalosis include pulmonary embolism, pneumonia, pulmonary edema, mechanical hyperventilation and interstitial lung disease. These drive hyperventilation and lower carbon dioxide. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"If the primary acid-base problem is metabolic, how is it compensated?","choices":["Potassium is excreted in the urine","Chloride is retained by the gut","Carbon dioxide is retained or excreted by breathing","Bicarbonate is retained or excreted by the kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the primary problem is metabolic, compensation is respiratory: carbon dioxide is retained or excreted by changing ventilation. Renal bicarbonate handling compensates respiratory disorders. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What usually causes hypercapnia?","choices":["Excess production by the tissues","Excess excretion from hyperventilation","Loss of bicarbonate in the urine","Under-excretion from hypoventilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypercapnia is usually the result of under-excretion from hypoventilation, not excess carbon dioxide production. Hypocapnia is the reverse, usually excess excretion from hyperventilation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which variable does the respiratory mechanism control?","choices":["Serum albumin","Carbon dioxide","Plasma bicarbonate","Serum potassium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The respiratory mechanism controls carbon dioxide: increased ventilation lowers it and decreased ventilation raises it. The renal mechanism is the one that controls plasma bicarbonate. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which electrolyte pattern results from renal compensation for respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Low bicarbonate, hypokalemia, hyperchloremia","High bicarbonate, hyperkalemia, hypochloremia","Normal bicarbonate, normal potassium, normal chloride","High bicarbonate, hypokalemia, hypochloremia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In respiratory alkalosis the kidneys excrete bicarbonate in the urine and retain chloride. This gives a reduced serum bicarbonate, hypokalemia and hyperchloremia as the compensated pattern. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why is a carbon dioxide above the Winter prediction in metabolic acidosis a warning sign?","choices":["It confirms an elevated anion gap","It can signal impending respiratory failure","It means the acidosis is resolving","It means the kidneys are compensating"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A carbon dioxide higher than the Winter formula predicts means metabolic acidosis together with respiratory acidosis. This can be a sign of impending respiratory failure, so it is a warning. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is the normal range for arterial partial pressure of carbon dioxide?","choices":["22 to 26 mm Hg","36 to 44 mm Hg","40 to 52 mm Hg","80 to 100 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal arterial partial pressure of carbon dioxide is 36 to 44 mm Hg, with 40 as the working midpoint. Venous carbon dioxide runs higher, at 40 to 52 mm Hg, so the arterial sample is the one that uses 36 to 44. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which breathing pattern is the profound compensation for metabolic acidosis in diabetic ketoacidosis?","choices":["Kussmaul respirations","Slow, shallow breathing","Breath holding","Normal quiet breathing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic acidosis is compensated by increased ventilation that lowers carbon dioxide. In severe cases such as diabetic ketoacidosis it is profound: Kussmaul respirations, with increased tidal volume and tachypnea. — PAJ5500 CMSI Acid Base MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"}]};
